WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.770
<v Narrator>So now let's talk about a service</v>

2
00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.450
that is named after what it does.

3
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.430
It is AWS Batch.

4
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.400
So Batch is a fully managed Batch processing service

5
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.340
that can allow you to do Batch processing at any scale.

6
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.540
And with the Batch service,

7
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:13.950
you can efficiently run hundreds

8
00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.840
of thousands of computing Batch jobs

9
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:18.030
on AWS very easily.

10
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:19.530
So what is a Batch job?

11
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:23.100
Well, a Batch job is a job that has a start and an end.

12
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.270
And that is opposed to,

13
00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.550
say, a continuous or a streaming job

14
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.260
that really doesn't ever end.

15
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:29.670
It's always running.

16
00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.110
But a Batch job, say for example,

17
00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.500
starts at 1:00 a.m. and finishes at 3:00 a.m.

18
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.010
So a Batch job has a point of time when it happens.

19
00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.020
And so the Batch service

20
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.390
will dynamically launch EC2 instances

21
00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:45.930
or Spot instances to accommodate with the load

22
00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.510
that you have to run these Batch jobs.

23
00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.720
So Batch will provision the right amount of compute

24
00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.840
and memory for you to deal with your Batch queue,

25
00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.400
and you just submit

26
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.620
or schedule Batch jobs into that Batch queue

27
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.800
and the Batch service does the rest.

28
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.470
Now, you define a Batch job using a Docker image,

29
00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.380
and then you can run it on either ECS, EKS,

30
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.390
or the Fargate service.

31
00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.580
And this is going to be very helpful to use Batch

32
00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.990
to run these Batch jobs.

33
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.840
And because it automatically scales the right number

34
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:16.920
of EC2 instances

35
00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:19.650
or Spot instances to do these jobs,

36
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.810
then you get lots of cost optimizations

37
00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:24.270
and you focus a lot less on the infrastructure.

38
00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.490
You just focus on your Batch jobs.

39
00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.680
So this should be more than enough for going to the exam.

40
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.800
But I just wanna show you a small diagram that I made.

41
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.100
So for example, say we wanted to process images submitted

42
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.130
by users into Amazon S3 in a Batch way.

43
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:41.340
So image will be put into Amazon S3,

44
00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.250
and this would trigger a Batch job.

45
00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:47.430
And so Batch would automatically have an ECS cluster made

46
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.220
of EC2 instances or Spot instances.

47
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.290
And Batch would make sure that you have the right amount

48
00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.630
of instances to accommodate with a load

49
00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.730
of Batch jobs you have in the Batch queue.

50
00:01:56.730 --> 00:02:00.660
And then these instances will be running your Docker images

51
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.340
that will be doing your job,

52
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:03.570
and then maybe that job will be

53
00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.580
to insert the processed object.

54
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.720
Maybe it's a filter on top

55
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.960
of the image into another Amazon S3 buckets.

56
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.610
So the question you may have is what is the difference

57
00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.630
between Batch and Lambda because it looks similar?

58
00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.110
So Lambda has a time limit,

59
00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.780
it's 15 minutes and you only get access

60
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.450
to a few programming languages on top of it.

61
00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:24.390
You have limited temporary dis space if you want

62
00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:25.470
to run your jobs,

63
00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:26.802
and it's going to be serverless,

64
00:02:26.802 --> 00:02:28.710
whereas Batch is very different.

65
00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:30.150
So Batch has no time limit

66
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.940
because it relies on EC2 instances.

67
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:35.730
It's any runtime that you want as long as you package it

68
00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.230
as a Docker image.

69
00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.620
And for storage, you rely on the storage that comes

70
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.090
with an EC2 instance.

71
00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:43.470
So it could be an EBS volume

72
00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.933
or an EC2 instance store for desk space,

73
00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.200
which can be a lot more than for Lambda functions.

74
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.840
And then finally, Batch is not a serverless service,

75
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.540
it's a managed service, but it relies on actual EC2

76
00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.070
instances being created.

77
00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.199
But these EC2 instances are managed by AWS,

78
00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:00.240
so we don't need

79
00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.400
to worry about the auto-scaling and so on.

80
00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.360
So I hope that was helpful

81
00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.310
and I will see you in the next lecture.

